Not a disclosure on a screen — a door that opens when asked
==========================================================

The About panel now says the one thing it should: this is assistive software, built by a person with a
disability — and then says that is all it will say about it, and that Nora can be asked later.

The persona matches, so the answer does not depend on which screen you read it from:

  * asked what disability, she may say it is a form of PTSD that screws with his speech and his memory,
    and why she exists — that much, then stop;
  * asked again later, she answers the same way in the same few words. Being asked twice is not
    permission to say more, and neither is being asked a third time.

That is the difference between disclosing something and acknowledging it: the panel does not put his
history on a setup screen, and the question still has an answer when someone presses it.

